# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for rcc
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* The back end

The direction is taken: c1 is rcc's only back end, it writes x86-64
and aarch64 assembly itself, and the system cc preprocesses,
assembles and links. The reasons are =docs/findings.md= and
=docs/llvm.md=, the design is =docs/backend.md= with its calls made
in =docs/design-calls.md=. The chapter holds c1's own defects on both
targets; the second target's design is =docs/aarch64.md=.

** TODO A returned ~?:~ whose arms store through a call's result moves each into the result register

A returned conditional joins in the result register: on aarch64 at
the top of the value, and below a cast, a comma or an assignment
where its arms take the name (=aarch64.md= 6.1); on x86-64 wherever
the return's name reaches it. Two arms that store through a call's
result, whose place holds that register at the store, are each moved
there: ~return c ? (*g() = a) : (*h() = b);~ is =mov w0, w1= on each
path on aarch64 and =movl %ecx, %eax= on x86-64, where a join in the
arms' register and the one move after it is an instruction fewer. On
x86-64 under a widening, ~return (long) (c ? ... : ...);~, the moves
and =movslq %eax, %rax= after the join are two instructions more than
=movslq %ecx, %rax=. =conditional.c='s =joined_called= on both targets
and =widened_called= on x86-64 have it.

Fix: none taken. The join in the arms' register is the shorter only
where both arms certainly miss the register, and whether a call's
result still holds it at the store depends on the arithmetic around
the call: ~p[f(i) + 1] = x~ holds it, ~p[i + f(j)] = x~ does not.

Check: =joined_called= with no move on its paths, and no function
longer.

** TODO x86-64: a spilled left operand of a row that commutes is reloaded, then used

~return g + bump();~ reloads the left operand its call spilled and
then adds it, =movq -8(%rbp), %rcx; addl %ecx, %eax=, where the slot
as the instruction's memory operand, =addl -8(%rbp), %eax=, is one
instruction. 370 rows over =tests= have the shape, of =add=, =imul=,
=and=, =or= and =xor=, under any consumer. A row of 32 bits would
read at 4 bytes a spill slot written at 8; the slot is no object of
the program, so G10 does not speak of it.

Fix: none taken; decide whether a spill slot may be read narrower
than it was written, then the left operand in its slot is the source
of a row that writes the right operand's register.

Check: the count over =tests=, and no function longer.

** TODO aarch64: a constant under a cast is added where the access's displacement could carry it

~((int *) (p + 1))[3]~ on a ~char *p~ is =add x0, x0, #1; ldr w0,
[x0, #12]=, where the two constants are one displacement, =ldr w0,
[x0, #13]=; a chain of casts adds each of its constants, three =add=
before the load in =two_casts()=, and a constant before a variable
index is added before the index where it could follow the sum,
=add x0, x0, #4= in =byte_both_sides()= for =ldrsh w0, [x0, #6]=
after it. x86-64 joins them (=denoted()= in =src/c1-x86-64.cc=,
=backend.md= 6.3). =view_subscript()=, =two_casts()= and
=byte_both_sides()= of =subscript-displacement= pin the adds, and
the =kview_index_*= rows of the pointers probe have the first.

Fix: the access's rows sum the constants through the casts into the
one displacement, where it is in reach after the sum.

Check: those three functions with no =add= of a constant, the count
over =tests=, and no function longer.

** TODO x86-64: the stop for an index in memory at a stride of 1 is written twice

=cast_sum()= and =memory_indexed()= in =src/c1-x86-64.cc= each test
that a sum at a stride of 1 has an index that is a 64-bit object in
memory, which the sum's =addq= takes as its operand.

Fix: =cast_sum()= calls =memory_indexed()=.

Check: one test, and no listing changes.

*p~ is =addq $1, %rax; movslq 4(%rax), %rax=, where =movslq 5(%rax),
%rax= is one instruction: the subscript's pointer is the cast,
generated as a value; the 80 =kview_index_*= rows of the pointers
probe have it. ~*(short *) ((char *) p + i * 4 + 2)~ is =addq %rcx,
%rax; movswl 2(%rax), %eax=, where the index joins the operand,
=movswl 2(%rax,%rcx,1), %eax=: =byte_after_index()= of
=subscript-displacement=.

Fix: =constant_indexed()= and =index_base()= take their pointer
through =cast_sum()=, the inner sum's rows forming a base, a
displacement and an index.

Check: =kview_index_*= one instruction shorter, =byte_after_index()=
with the index in the operand, and no function longer.

** TODO A name's address plus a constant under a cast does not fold into the slot or the label

~*(int *) ((char *) &s + 4)~ on a local is =leaq -20(%rbp), %rax;
movl 4(%rax), %eax= and =add x0, x29, #20; ldr w0, [x0, #4]=, where
the slot's own operand, =movl -16(%rbp), %eax= and =ldr w0, [x29,
#24]=, is one instruction: =lvalue_offset()= in =src/c1-common.cc=
does not read through a cast between pointer types, and aarch64's
=access_sum()= leaves a name's address plus a constant to it. On
aarch64 =byte_slot()= and =byte_label()= of =subscript-displacement=
and =main= of =g11-misaligned= form the address first for the same
reason.

Fix: =lvalue_offset()= reads through a cast between pointer types,
its offsets in bytes.

Check: a cost function for the shape on a slot and on a label, those
three aarch64 listings one instruction shorter, an offset that is no
multiple of the access's size assembling, =ldr w0, [x29, #21]=, and
no function longer.

** TODO aarch64: a narrow or widened prefix step's value is stepped outside the register variable

~r = ++c~ on a =char= in memory into a ~register int r~ is =ldrb w0,
[x29, #24]; add w0, w0, #1; strb w0, [x29, #24]; uxtb w19, w0=, and
~l = ++x~ on an =int= into a =register long= ends =sxtw x19, w0=:
the step is in an expression register, and the conversion's
extension writes the variable. The load and the step in the
variable, =ldrb w19; add w19, w19, #1; strb w19; uxtb w19, w19=, is
the same count and takes no expression register, but the variable's
register then holds a value outside its representation from the add
to the extension, which chapter 4 of =aarch64.md= does not allow
today. 23 forms over =tests=; x86-64 keeps the same split.

Fix: none taken; it moves the cast's rule on what a register
variable's register may hold.

Check: the count over =tests=, and no function longer.

** TODO A 32-bit write's value widened as an unsigned is cleared again

Any write of a 32-bit register name clears the upper half, so the
value is valid to 64 bits as an =unsigned=. A signed value no load
wrote keeps its clearing move all the same: ~(long) (unsigned)
narrow()~ on a call's ~signed char~ result is =movsbl %al, %eax; movl
%eax, %eax= and =sxtb w0, w0; mov w0, w0= (=unsigned_called()= of
=widen=); =addl $3, %eax; movl %eax, %eax= is written 4 times in
=test_writes= of =tests/probe/statics-main.c= on each target, with 3
joins beside them; and on x86-64 a =switch= on a 32-bit sum clears
its table's index, =addl $1, %eax; cmpl $4, %eax; ja; movl %eax,
%eax=, 20 sites over =tests=. The record of a value's valid width is
by its type's signedness, so a signed =int= valid to 32 bits is a
32-bit write of the generator's, a 64-bit value narrowed, which
writes nothing, or a call's result, whose upper half the convention
leaves undefined, and a conversion to =unsigned= cannot tell them
apart. ~(long) (unsigned) whole()~ on an =int= call must keep its
move (=unsigned_int_called()= of =widen=).

Fix: none taken. It needs a record of its own on a held value, that
its register's upper half is zero: set by every 32-bit write, lost
at a call's result, a narrowing and a 64-bit write, kept by a spill
and its reload, true past a join only where every path has it, and
never a register variable's register. That touches most rows of both
generators for the sites above.

Check: those sites with no clearing move, =unsigned_int_called()=
with its own, and no function longer.

* Guarantees

The promises in =docs/guarantees.md=. The entry number is the
guarantee it belongs to. All are decided; G15 is withdrawn with its
number reserved. c1 writes none of the guard of G4, the round trip
of G8 and the laundering of G16, which draft 1 designed against an
optimizer, because nothing between the source and the instruction
reasons about the program.

** TODO No document gathers the implementation-defined choices

4p10 asks for "a document that defines all implementation-defined
and locale-specific characteristics and all extensions". rcc fixes
each choice per target in its code, and the documents record them
piecemeal: plain =char='s signedness on aarch64 in =docs/aarch64.md=,
the extent of =register= in =docs/cost.md= chapter 1, the narrowing
and the right shift of a negative value in G2 and G3.
=docs/guarantees.md= and =docs/rationale.md= say that no table
gathers them.

Fix: one table, per target, of the items of Annex J.3 that the
implemented subset reaches, each with its choice and the code or pin
that fixes it; the "Not in this list" of both documents points at it.
Among them what an access to a volatile object is (6.7.4.1p8): each
load and each store the source writes, at the object's width, which
=shape.md= chapter 5 says and =tests/cost/volatile.c= pins. And the
integer type compatible with each enumerated type (J.3.10(7)): by
its values, =unsigned int=, =unsigned long=, =int= or =long=, as
=clang= chooses on both targets, which =shape.md= chapter 5 says and
the =enums= probe pins; an enumeration under =packed= is refused.
And the encodings and the wide types: a literal of no prefix is its
bytes in UTF-8 (J.3.5(6)), a wide one UTF-32 (J.3.5(7)); =wchar_t=
is =int= on x86-64 and =unsigned int= on aarch64, =char16_t= and
=char32_t= =unsigned short= and =unsigned int=; a constant of no
prefix with several characters is a not-yet, and a wide one with
several an error (J.3.5(9), (10)). =shape.md= chapters 2 and 3 and
=aarch64.md= chapter 11 say so.

Check: every J.3 item the subset reaches has a row for each target,
and each row agrees with a pin or a probe.

** TODO =guarantees.md= names each guarantee's instruction for x86-64 alone

The page's opening says each entry gives "the instruction that
delivers it (=backend.md= chapter 9)", and the entries name x86-64's:
=movb=, =movsbl=, =lea=, =cqto=, a displacement off =rbp=, =call
f@PLT=. The aarch64 instructions are the table of =aarch64.md=
chapter 9, which the page does not name. A reader on aarch64 finds
another machine's instructions and no pointer to its own.

Fix: the opening says the instruction clauses are x86-64's and names
=aarch64.md= chapter 9 for the other target; or each clause names
both.

Check: a reader on aarch64 is sent to the table of its instructions.

* Cost model

The record in =docs/cost.md=: what each construct compiles to under
c1, its table written from the pins of =tests/cost/=. The findings of
the measurement (=docs/findings.md=) were facts about LLVM's code
generator and left with it; that page's chapter 8 says what became
of each. The entries below are rows the table lacks.

** TODO Chapter 4's x86-64 numbers predate the listings of its programs

The x86-64 table of =cost.md= chapter 4 was measured before a
remainder's value became the value in =rdx=. That step takes one
instruction, =movq %rdx, %rax=, out of the step loop of ~rcc
collatz.c~, the loop the cell times: 2293 ms, 7.75. The other
listings of =tests/perf= it changes, it changes outside their hot
loops, and the loops of =tests/slot= are the same. The operand in
memory taken by a multiply by a constant and by a cast of
signedness changes every x86-64 listing of =tests/perf= again, by
one to five instructions, outside the loops the chapter times: the
step of each program's generator and its checksum. An address
written into a =register= variable's register takes one =movq= out
of =matmul-tuned= before its inner loop, whose head is then at
0x1320, in the second half of its 64-byte line. The =leaq= of ~pb
+= n~ written into the variable's register takes an instruction out
of that loop, which is nine instructions and 29 bytes; the chapter
states the listing of today, and its matmul cell, 572 ms, and the
rows of 4.2, which names a loop of 32 bytes, are of the loop of ten. A
constant on the left taken as one on the right shortens the step
loop of collatz on x86-64, as written by one instruction, =imulq $3,
-48(%rbp), %rax=, and as tuned by two, =imulq $3, %r14, %r14; addq
$1, %r14=: the cells 2293 and 473 ms are of the longer loops. A
multiply of a =register= variable by a constant, now the one
three-operand =imul=, takes an instruction out of the checksum loop
of ~rcc strings-tuned.c~, =imull $31, %ebx, %eax=, which runs once
for each string.

A sum whose right operand holds a call, written into the register
its consumer names, takes one instruction out of the path of ~rcc
fib.c~ that recurses, which the fib cell times.

Fix: =make bench= three times on the machine of the table, and the
table and what is drawn from it taken again.

Check: the table is of a run made after the last change to a hot
loop of its programs.

** TODO The Oryon's numbers of chapter 4 predate the listings of its programs

The Oryon's table of =cost.md= chapter 4, its column in 4.3 and in
=slot.md=, and the aarch64 alignment probe of 4.2, on the Oryon and
on Firestorm, were measured on 2026-11-13. The aarch64 listings of
collatz and strings as written, and of collatz, matmul, sieve, sort
and strings as tuned, have changed since; the slot loops have not.
The chapter says so under the table. The sentence of 4.3 that the
Zen 2 slot times keep their ratios to =rr='s within 1% with the boost
on was measured before the x86-64 loops of =sr= and =ss= changed.

Fix: =make bench= three times on the Oryon; the aarch64 probe of 4.2
again on both cores; one ~make slots CORE=1~ on Zen 2 with the boost
on.

Check: every cell of those tables and lists is of a run made after
the last change to its listing.

** TODO The M1 Max's fib cell of chapter 4 predates its listing

The M1 Max's table of =cost.md= chapter 4 was measured before a
=return='s value was computed in =x0= on aarch64. That takes one
instruction, =mov w0, w1=, out of the path of ~rcc fib.c~ that
recurses, 24 instructions where the cell timed 25. =fib-tuned= and
the other programs' timed loops are the same.

Fix: =make bench= three times on the M1 Max, and the fib row and
what is drawn from it taken again.

Check: the cell is of a run made after the last change to fib's
listing.

** TODO =slot.md= says a round trip costs "at least six times" the loop

=slot.md= 4.3, "The round trip's price", says =rs= runs at least six
times as long as =rr=. From the integer times it is 1719 ms above
=rr= against 6 x 287 = 1722 on Zen 2, and 1757 against 1764 on the
Oryon: 5.99 and 5.98 times. The figure holds at two decimals of a
cycle only.

Fix: "about six times".

Check: =rs= less =rr= against six times =rr=, from the integer ms of
each core.

** TODO =cost.md= says nothing writes an uninitialized register variable

The rows of 2.1 and 2.2 for a =register= local without an initializer
say "nothing writes it until the program does". For a =char=, a
=short= or an =unsigned= variable the declaration writes the
extension in the variable's own register, =movl %ebx, %ebx=,
=movsbl %bl, %ebx=, =mov w19, w19=, =uxtb w19, w19=, as the G5 rows
of =backend.md= and =aarch64.md= state.

Fix: the two rows except the extension, as the G5 rows do.

Check: the rows agree with the listing of ~register unsigned u;~ and
of ~register char c;~ on both targets.

** TODO =cost.md= 4.1 does not state the metric of its table

4.1 sets the ratios of the LLVM pipeline's table beside those of
chapter 4's x86-64 table. The pipeline's table was taken in user time
alone; chapter 4's is user plus system. Each ratio is taken within
its own table, so the comparison stands, but 4.1 never says how its
table was timed and a reader takes the two as one method.

Fix: one clause in 4.1's opening that gives its metric.

Check: 4.1 states its metric, and it is the one its table was taken
with.

** TODO No row pins a multiply or an unsigned division by a power of two

c1 writes =x * 4= as =imull $4, %eax, %eax=, and an unsigned =x / 4=
and =x % 4= as =movl $4, %ecx; xorl %edx, %edx; divl %ecx=, the
remainder then moved from =edx= (=backend.md= 6.4). This is the
thesis's answer to finding 2 of =findings.md=, the selector's
strength reduction. But no cost program pins the three: =mul-three=
multiplies by 3 and =div-four= divides a signed value, so a change to
c1 that turned the three into a shift or a mask would show in no
row.

Fix: a cost program pinning =imull $4=, the =movl $4= into a
register and the =divl= of the unsigned division and remainder.

Check: the row and its pin.

** TODO No aarch64 pin shows the extension of a signed byte

Plain =char= is unsigned on aarch64 (=aarch64.md= chapter 11), so a
=char= of the cost corpus is extended there with =uxtb=: =cast='s
=signed_()= and =narrow='s =f()= show the unsigned forms on that
target. =widen= pins the signed load, =ldrsb=, of a =signed char= in
memory. No cost program extends one held in a register, a call's
result or a register variable, so no aarch64 pin has =sxtb=, and a
change to that extension would turn no row red. The probes run the
type; the pins do not show its listing.

Fix: a cost program with a =signed char= call result and register
variable widened, pinned for both targets.

Check: its aarch64 pin has =sxtb=, and perturbing it in
=c1-aarch64.cc= turns the row red.

** TODO The tl;dr says a register variable is never stored

=docs/cost.md='s tl;dr has a =register= variable "never stored", and
=README.md= has "a variable that is never stored". The variable has
no slot and no store at a write. But its register is stored to a
spill slot before a branch one of whose paths writes it while a read
of it is held (=backend.md= 6.7): =movq %rbx, -24(%rbp)= and =str
x19, [x29, #32]= in the two pins of =g13-read-across-branch=.

Fix: "has no slot of its own" in both places, with =backend.md= 6.7
named for the save.

Check: the sentence agrees with both pins of =g13-read-across-branch=.

* Tests

** TODO =tests/oracle --pin= says nothing of a pin that is the same

=tests/costs --pin= prints a line for every pin, new, changed or the
same. =tests/oracle --pin= prints one only for a new or a changed
pin, so a clean run adopts in silence and the two scripts read
differently.

Fix: the oracle prints "pinned <file> (same)" too.

Check: =tests/oracle --pin= on a clean tree prints one line for each
program.

** TODO The oracle's time limit cannot tell 124 from a timeout

GNU =timeout= exits 124 for a run it stopped and passes the program's
own status otherwise, so a program that exits 124 by itself is
recorded as =timeout=. And =timeout= sends SIGTERM, which a program
may ignore; =timeout -k= would follow with SIGKILL.

Fix: =timeout -k 1 10=, and a corpus rule that no program exits 124.

Check: a program that ignores SIGTERM ends its row.

** TODO A signal death records as 128 + n, like an exit with 128 + n

A program killed by signal n is recorded as 128 + n: =timeout= dies
of the child's signal, and the shell reports 128 + n. So the G9
program's SIGSEGV is the row's 139, and a program that exits 139 by
itself records the same.

Fix: show a status above 128 by its signal name, =SEGV= from =kill
-l=, in the table and in =expected=, and a corpus rule that no
program exits above 128.

Check: the =g9-null-load= row reads =SEGV= and passes.

** TODO =tests/probes= has no time limit

A probe program that loops hangs the step: =tests/probes= runs each
program with no limit, where the oracle gives a program 10 seconds. A
generator fault that turns a loop's exit wrong leaves the program
running and =make test= waiting on it.

Fix: a time limit per probe program, as the oracle's, and the
program's death by it a failed step.

Check: a probe made to loop shows as a failed step, and =make test=
ends.

** TODO A signal death leaves a core file

The oracle runs each program with the caller's core limit. Where it is
unlimited, every run of =g9-null-load= under rcc leaves a core file
where =core_pattern= says: in the tree's root where the pattern is
=core=, as =core.<pid>=, and in =/tmp= where it is
=/tmp/core.%e.%p.%s=, about 290 KB each; and =timeout= says "the
monitored command dumped core" in the log.

Fix: =ulimit -c 0= in the script before the runs.

Check: =make test= leaves no new =core.*= in the tree's root and none
in =/tmp=.

** TODO A G14 corpus program needs a non-constant condition

=for (;;);= hangs under =clang -O2= as well: C11 6.8.5p6 exempts a
loop whose controlling expression is constant from the
forward-progress assumption. The divergence G14 promises shows only
on a loop like =while (x);= with =x= a non-volatile object the loop
never writes.

Fix: write that program as the G14 entry, expected =timeout= under
rcc and 0 under clang.

Check: the oracle row.

** TODO The register rows pin only part of c1's register rules

The five programs that declare =register= pin the registers in
declaration order, the pushes with their unwind offsets, a register
parameter's move from its argument register, a read as an operand, a
copy before an instruction that writes, an assignment, a constant's
immediate, and G5's unwritten register. No program of either corpus
shows the rest of =backend.md= chapter 4, so a change to any of these
shows in no pin:

- a compound assignment or a =++= on the register in place, =addl $1,
  %r13d=;
- a narrow register variable: the extending store =movsbl %al,
  %ebx=, the re-extension after a step, and an unwritten one's
  extension at its declaration;
- the copy of a held read before a write, =i + i++=, and before the
  paths of =&&=, =||= and =?:= (G13);
- a register parameter past the sixth, loaded from its stack slot,
  and the order of the moves and the stores among mixed parameters;
- the epilogue's =leaq -8k(%rbp), %rsp= before the pops, which only a
  function with pushes and a =subq= writes;
- the sixth live variable in a slot, and a later block taking =rbx=
  again.

Fix: a cost program for each, with its pin.

Check: perturbing each rule in =c1-x86-64.cc= turns a row red.

** TODO =-o -= names a file, not standard output

=rcc -S -o -= writes a file called =-= in the working directory. Every
cc reads =-o -= as standard output for =-S=, and a reader of the
assembly expects it.

Fix: with =-S=, an output of =-= writes the text to standard output;
with =-c= or a link it stays a usage error.

Check: =rcc -S -o - x.c | head -1= prints the first line of the
assembly and leaves no file.

** TODO Two corpus programs describe the x86-64 listing only

The comments of =g7-ptr-equal= and =g11-misaligned= in =tests/corpus=
say what c1 writes by x86-64's names: "adjacent slots off rbp", and
"by one movl: x86 has one mov for every alignment". Each has an
aarch64 pin beside its x86-64 one, with slots off =x29= and an =ldr=,
and the comment is true of one pin of the two. =g5-register-undef=
and the three =g13-= programs name both targets' instructions.

Fix: each comment names both targets' instructions, as those four
do.

Check: no comment of =tests/corpus= names a register or an
instruction of one target alone.

** TODO =tests/slots= reads the clock of an idle core

The script reads =scaling_cur_freq= once, before the first timed
run, and computes the cycle column at that frequency. The core is
idle then. On a core with a frequency boost the reading is not the
clock a run gets: on the Zen 2 core with the boost on it read 3853
and 3593 MHz in two series, and =rr= came out at 0.84 and 0.78
cycles an iteration, under the one cycle its add takes. The script
prints the column all the same; the only sign is that the reading
after the last run differs, which its header mentions and its output
does not flag.

Fix: read the frequency while a run is in flight, or leave the cycle
columns out and say why when the two readings differ by more than a
percent.

Check: on an x86-64 core with its boost on, =make slots= prints =rr=
at about one cycle an iteration, or prints no cycle column and says
why.

** TODO The C sources of the tests do not follow =.clang-format=

=clang-format --dry-run tests/perf/*.c= flags all twelve: each keeps
a definition's return type on the line of its name, and =collatz*.c=
and =sort*.c= align their =#define= lines otherwise than the tree's
=.clang-format= does. The two files of =tests/perf/aarch64= follow
it, so they differ from their shared siblings by that layout.

The two corpora are laid out as the twelve are: =clang-format
--dry-run= prints 112 warnings over =tests/cost/*.c= and 24 over
=tests/corpus/*.c=, each program keeping a definition's return type
on the line of its name. The C sources of the tests follow
=.clang-format=, with one difference from the C++ sources: C is
indented by 8 spaces, C++ by 4. The file is written for C++ alone and
has no section for C.

Fix: a section for C in =.clang-format=, the C++ rules with an
indentation of 8; then every C source under =tests/= laid out by it.
No listing changes. A pin of =tests/error= changes in the line and
the column of its diagnostic and in nothing else, and a program that
is malformed on purpose is laid out by hand where the formatter
cannot read it.

Check: =clang-format --dry-run= over every =.c= and =.h= under
=tests/= prints nothing, every listing is byte-identical, and =make
test= passes.

** TODO The shared =matmul-tuned.c= says every loop ends on =!==

The header of =tests/perf/matmul-tuned.c= says "Every loop ends on
!=". The loops of the fill, the repetitions and the checksum end on
=<=; the three loops of the product end on =!==. The aarch64 file
says "Every loop of the product".

Fix: the shared header says it as the aarch64 one does.

Check: the header read against the loops.

** TODO The aarch64 =strings-tuned.c= draws a warning from =clang=

=clang -O2= warns at =copy()= of =tests/perf/aarch64/strings-tuned.c=,
"using the result of an assignment as a condition without
parentheses", on ~while (*d++ = *s++)~, and every =make bench= on
aarch64 writes the warning to =build/perf/log=. Writing the test as
~!= 0~ would silence it but is another program under rcc, which
compares where a comparison is written.

Fix: a second pair of parentheses, ~while ((*d++ = *s++))~.

Check: =make bench= on aarch64 writes no warning to the log, and the
listing of =copy()='s loop under rcc is unchanged.

* Parser

** TODO A warning recorded before an error is never printed

The parser records a warning in the unit, and the driver prints the
unit's warnings only after a successful parse. =error_t= ends the
parse at the first error, so the unit and its warnings are lost with
the exception. Thus =&k= on a sixth register variable reports the
error alone: the warning that put =k= in a slot was recorded at its
declaration, before the error, and is never printed.

Fix: the driver prints the warnings the parser recorded before the
error too. Since the unit is lost with the exception, the warnings
need a home the exception does not destroy, such as a list the
driver passes to the parser.

Check: =int f(void) { register int a, b, c, d, e, k; return *&k; }=
prints the warning at =k= and then the error at =&=, exit status 1.

** TODO Assignment to a const object is not diagnosed

The parser accepts =const= and drops it: the type is the machine's
view and carries no qualifier. So =const int c = 1; c = 2;= compiles,
though 6.5.17.1p2 requires a modifiable lvalue and 6.3.2.1p1 excludes
a const-qualified one; a conforming compiler must diagnose it. The
same silence covers a =const= pointee assigned through, and a
=T *= initialized from a =const T *=. =volatile= is dropped the same
way, and a =T *= initialized from a =volatile T *= is not diagnosed
either, though 6.5.17.2p1 asks that the left pointee have every
qualifier of the right.

Fix: qualifiers on the symbol and on the pointee, checked by
=require_modifiable_lvalue= and by the assignment conversion; the
type itself stays unqualified.

Check: the three programs above fail with a diagnostic; =const= in
every other position still parses.

** TODO A pointer to a type rcc does not honour takes no pointer to the type behind it

~typedef int word_t __attribute__ ((mode (word))); void f(word_t *);
void g(int *p) { f(p); }~ fails with "incompatible pointer types";
so does the same program with ~typedef _Atomic int a_t;~. The error
calls the program invalid where rcc has not looked: a typedef through
a marking attribute or =_Atomic= names a type of an identity of its
own, which keeps nothing of the type it was made from.

Fix: the refusal says "not yet" and names the typedef's words, where
one of the two pointees is such a type; or the type keeps what it was
made from.

Check: the first program is refused as not yet, or compiles.

** TODO A type rcc does not honour cannot be written twice

~typedef int w __attribute__ ((mode (word))); typedef int w
__attribute__ ((mode (word)));~ fails with "redefinition of typedef
'w' with another type". A typedef may be declared again as the same
type, and these two are. Each declaration through an attribute rcc
does not honour makes a type of an identity of its own, so the
second is never the first. Three more are made the same way and fail
the same way: a typedef through =_Atomic=; the atomic type specifier,
so that ~void f(_Atomic(int) *); void f(_Atomic(int) *);~ fails with
"conflicting types for 'f'"; and a typedef of an array whose size rcc
cannot compute, ~typedef int a[sizeof (struct s)];~ written twice.

Fix: a type made again by the same means from the same type takes
the earlier one's identity.

Check: the programs above compile; with =mode (byte)= in the second
typedef the first still fails.

** TODO A marking attribute on a later declaration does not reach an earlier call

~int f(void); int g(void) { return f(); } int f(void) __attribute__
((weak));~ compiles. The attribute marks the symbol from where it is
written, and the call parsed before it was accepted. GNU C lets a
later declaration add an attribute, and the use rcc cannot honour has
then been taken.

Fix: decide whether an attribute rcc does not honour, met after a
use of the symbol, is refused where it is written.

Check: the program above is refused, at the attribute or at the
call.

** TODO The other target is preprocessed with this machine's headers

=rcc -target x86-64= on an aarch64 machine runs =clang -E
--target=x86_64-linux-gnu=, which searches =/usr/include=: the
aarch64 glibc's headers, read with x86-64's predefined macros. For
=stdio.h= the two texts are the same here, so the pin of
=header-stdio= for the other target is honest; a header that differs
by architecture, =bits/= and =asm/= above all, would give the other
target this machine's declarations.

Fix: say in =shape.md= chapter 8 that a program with system headers
is compiled for the machine's own target, and refuse =-target= for
another machine when the program includes one; or take a sysroot.

Check: the documents and the driver agree on what =-target= does to
=#include <...>=.

** TODO A block's declaration that begins with an attribute is read as an expression

~int main(void) { __attribute__ ((unused)) int x = 1; return x; }~
fails with "expected expression" at the attribute. At file scope the
same declaration is read. =is_declaration_start()= does not take the
attribute keyword, so a block reads the tokens as a statement.

Fix: =is_declaration_start()= takes the attribute keyword; the
attribute is then read among the specifiers, dropped or not yet on a
local by its kind.

Check: the program above compiles and returns 1; with ~__attribute__
((aligned (16)))~ it is refused as not yet, by the attribute's name.

** TODO A call through a pointer passes a parameter a marking attribute marked

~int f(int x __attribute__ ((mode (word))));~ marks =f=, and a call
of it is refused, "not yet: attribute 'mode'": rcc would pass the
argument as an =int= where the attribute asks for another size. The
same parameter in a pointer's prototype is not refused: ~int call(int
(*p)(int x __attribute__ ((mode (word))))) { return p(1); }~ compiles,
and the call passes an =int=. The mark is the symbol's; a function
type has none, so the attribute is lost on a pointer's prototype and
on a typedef of function type.

Fix: the function type carries the marking, so that a call through a
pointer to it is refused as a call of a marked function is.

Check: the second program above is refused by the attribute's name.

** TODO An error after the first not-yet in a typedef's array size is not seen

~typedef int a[sizeof (struct s) + undeclared];~ compiles: in a
typedef, an array size that needs what rcc does not have yet makes a
type rcc declares and does not use, and the parse of the size ends at
its first refusal, so the undeclared name after it is never read.

Fix: the rest of the size is parsed for its errors after the refusal;
or the documents say that a size rcc cannot compute is not checked.

Check: the typedef above fails with "'undeclared' undeclared".

** TODO A parameter of a typedef's array type rcc cannot size is not a pointer

~typedef long g_t[sizeof (struct s)]; void f(g_t g); void f(long
*g);~ fails with "conflicting types for 'f'". A parameter of array
type is adjusted to a pointer to the element, and =g_t= is an array
of =long=; but the typedef names a type rcc does not use, which is
no array in the tree, so nothing is adjusted. glibc declares no such
parameter in the headers surveyed.

Fix: the type keeps its element, so that a parameter of it is
adjusted.

Check: the program above compiles.

** TODO =_Atomic ( type-name )= takes an array and a function type

~typedef _Atomic(int [3]) a_t;~ and ~typedef _Atomic(int (void))
f_t;~ are accepted. 6.7.3.5p3 says the type name "shall not refer to
an array type, a function type, an atomic type, or a qualified
type". The specifier makes a type rcc declares and does not use and
looks at nothing inside the parentheses; a qualified type cannot be
told, since qualifiers are dropped.

Fix: the specifier refuses an array and a function type.

Check: two programs of =tests/error= with their pins.

** TODO A macro of =stdatomic.h= draws "undeclared"

~atomic_flag_test_and_set(p)~ fails with "'__c11_atomic_exchange'
undeclared": the header's macros expand to =clang='s builtin
functions, which rcc takes for identifiers. The program is valid, and
the message says a name is missing where an atomic access is not
yet.

Fix: a name that begins =__c11_atomic_= or =__atomic_= is refused as
not yet, by name.

Check: the call above fails with "not yet", pinned in =tests/error=.

** TODO =tgmath.h= does not parse

=clang='s =tgmath.h= stops at its line 34, ~static
_Argument_type_is_not_arithmetic __tg_promote(...) __attribute__
((__unavailable__, __overloadable__));~, "not yet: variadic function
without named parameters": a function with =(...)= for its whole
parameter list, overloaded by an attribute, which no other C header
does. More than a thousand overloaded declarations follow. Every
other header of the 58 surveyed parses.

Fix: decide whether =clang='s own headers are read: the type-generic
macros need overloading, which is not C; a program that includes
=tgmath.h= needs floating point first.

Check: the documents say what a program that includes =tgmath.h=
gets.

** TODO =[*]= in a parameter is "expected expression"

~void f(int n, int a[*]);~ fails with "expected expression" at the
=*=. It is valid in a declaration that is no definition: 6.7.7.3p4
lets =*= stand for the size of a variable length array of unspecified
size, at function prototype scope alone. A parameter's size that
names another parameter is taken as unspecified already; the spelling
that says so is not read.

Fix: =[*]= in a parameter's brackets is the size left out; in a
definition it is the error it is.

Check: the declaration above compiles; ~void f(int n, int a[*]) { }~
fails with a message about the =*=.

** TODO =static= and a qualifier in a parameter's brackets are read in one place only

A parameter's array declarator takes =static= and type qualifiers
inside its brackets, in the array type derivation the parameter is
adjusted from. The parser reads them in the suffix written right
after the parameter's name. Three spellings draw "expected
expression" where another message, or none, is due:

- ~int f(int (a)[static 3]);~ and ~int f(int n, int (a)[n]);~ are
  valid, the parentheses redundant, and are refused;
- ~int f(int a[3][const 4]);~ is an error, of a qualifier in an array
  derivation that is not the outermost (6.7.7.3p1), and the message
  does not say so;
- ~int f(int a[static]);~ is an error, of =static= without a size, and
  the message does not say so.

Fix: the suffix after redundant parentheses is the parameter's own;
the two errors take messages of their own.

Check: the first two declarations compile; the other two are pinned
in =tests/error= with their messages.

** TODO =(const void)= is taken as =(void)=

~int f(const void);~, ~int f(register void);~ and a parameter of a
typedef for =const void= declare a function of no parameters. The
empty list is "an unnamed parameter of type void as the only item"
(6.7.7.4p9), and a qualified =void=, or one with a storage class, is
not that; =clang= and =gcc= refuse each. rcc drops qualifiers where
it reads them and tests the parameter's type, so it cannot tell.

Fix: with qualifiers in the type model, the entry on =const=; the
storage class is refused where the list is taken as empty.

Check: the three declarations fail with "'void' must be the only
parameter".

** TODO A parameter that takes an enumeration constant's name is called a parameter twice

~int f(enum { A } x, int A);~ fails with "redefinition of parameter
'A'". It should fail: the constant and the parameter are declared in
one scope. But the first =A= is no parameter.

Fix: the message names what the name was first declared as.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with the declaration above, pinned
"redefinition of 'A'".

** TODO =static= in the declaration of a =for= is an error

~for (static int i = 0; i < 3; i++)~ fails with "'static' in the
declaration of a 'for'". C23 has no such constraint: C17's 6.8.5p3
allowed only =auto= and =register= there, and N3220's 6.8.6 says
nothing of the storage class; =gcc= and =clang= take it with
=-std=c23=. The program is valid in the dialect rcc cites and wrong
in the one it preprocesses in. A function declared there is taken,
as C23 and =clang= take it; =extern= and =static= are refused.
=static_assert= is of the same kind: a keyword in C23 and a macro of
=assert.h= in the dialect rcc preprocesses in, which is how rcc has
it; with C23 the lexer takes the keyword.
=u8= is of the same kind too: a =u8= string is an array of =char=
in that dialect and of =char8_t= in C23, and ~u8'x'~ is C23's alone,
refused as a not-yet.

Fix: decide the dialect's answer: the error stays with C17's
paragraph cited, or the declaration is a block's static.

Check: the pin of =static-in-for= agrees with the decision.

** TODO A =static= array never given a size has one element

~static int a[];~ at file scope, with no later declaration that
sizes it, is an array of one element at the end of the file, as a
tentative definition with external linkage is. 6.9.3p3 says the
declared type of a tentative definition with internal linkage "shall
not be an incomplete type"; =gcc= and =clang= warn and make it one
element too.

Fix: an error at the end of the file, as the linkage conflict of
6.2.2p7 is.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with the declaration, pinned with
the error.

** TODO =main= may be declared =static=

~static int main(void) { return 0; }~ compiles to a function local
to its object file, and the link fails with "undefined reference to
`main'". The linker's message names no line of the program.

Fix: =static= on a declaration of =main= is an error, where a
function specifier on one already is.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with the definition, pinned with
the error.

** TODO A =static= definition after a header's =static inline= one is "not yet"

A system header's =static inline= definition is accepted, its body
skipped, and the function left with a mark and no body. A program
that then defines a =static= function of that name gets "not yet:
static function 'f'" at its own definition. It is a redefinition, and
the message says something is missing: =function_definition()= asks
whether the function has a body before it asks for the mark, and the
skipped definition left none. Two definitions in the program's own
source draw "redefinition of 'f'".

Fix: the mark of a skipped definition counts as a definition where a
second one is met.

Check: a program of =tests/error= that marks its first definition as
a system header's, pinned "redefinition of 'f'".

** TODO A function specifier stands on a definition of =main=

~_Noreturn int main(void) { for (;;) { } }~ compiles, and ~inline
int main(void) { return 0; }~ fails with "not yet: 'inline' on a
function definition". 6.7.5p4 allows no function specifier in a
declaration of =main=; a declaration without a body and a =static
inline= definition are refused with "'inline' in a declaration of
'main'", and a plain definition is not asked.

Fix: =refuse_specifier_on_main()= is called for every definition.

Check: two programs of =tests/error= with the definitions above,
pinned with the error of =main=.

** TODO A string in parentheses initializes an array of char only without braces

~char s[] = ("abc");~ compiles, and ~char t[] = { ("abc") };~ fails
with "incompatible pointer to integer conversion". In a list the
string is known by its tokens, a string literal before a comma or the
closing brace; outside a list the parentheses are looked through.
=clang= takes both, and 6.7.11p15 says "a string literal", which a
parenthesized one is not in so many words.

Fix: one answer for both spellings: both taken, or both refused with
a message that names the parentheses.

Check: the two declarations behave alike, pinned in =tests/error= or
in =tests/cost=.

** TODO Two pairs of braces around a scalar's initializer are an error

~int n = { { 1 } };~ fails with "too many braces around scalar
initializer". 6.7.11p12 gives a scalar "a single expression,
optionally enclosed in braces", one pair, in its Semantics and not as
a constraint, so the program is undefined and not wrong; =clang= and
=gcc= compile it with a warning.

Fix: decide whether rcc keeps the error or takes the inner braces
with a warning.

Check: the pin of =global-initializer-scalar-braces= agrees with the
decision.

** TODO A typedef followed by a function body draws an error about a semicolon

~typedef int f(void) { return 0; }~ fails with "expected ';' after
top level declarator" at the brace. The program is wrong, and the
message does not say how: a typedef cannot define a function.

Fix: a message of its own where a typedef's function declarator
meets a brace.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with its pin.

** TODO The comments on typedef and extern cite no paragraph

The comments written with =typedef= and =extern= state their rules
without the standard's numbers, since no copy was at hand to verify
them against: a label's name space, the declaration of a =for=, the
storage class of a parameter, a typedef redeclared as the same type,
a typedef name as a type specifier, =extern= on a function, an
=extern= object without an initializer, a typedef's initializer. Two
existing citations were widened unverified: 6.2.7p2 to objects of
file scope, and 6.5.2p2 to "as an object or a function".

Fix: each rule cited by its paragraph in N3220, read from the text.

Check: every citation in the comments of =src/parse.cc= that this
entry names is found at its number in N3220.

** TODO An enumeration declared in a struct's member list declares nothing

~struct s { enum e { A, B } k; }; int f(void) { return B; }~ fails
with "'B' undeclared". The constants of an enumeration declared
among a struct's members have the scope the struct is declared in
(6.2.1p4), and =clang= compiles the program. The parser skips a
struct's member list whole, the enum specifier with it.

Fix: the slice that reads member lists declares them; until then the
skip parses each enum specifier it meets outside parentheses.

Check: the program compiles and =f= returns 1.

** TODO A forward reference to an enumeration is refused

~enum e; enum e *p;~ before the list of =e= fails with "enum 'e'
used before its list". That is the constraint of 6.7.3.4p2, but
=clang= takes it without a word as an extension, and a header may
write it. A type in the tree is a value with no identity of its tag,
so a pointer made before the list cannot become a pointer to the
integer type the list completes.

Fix: with the identity of a tag that structs need (~struct s *p;~
before its members), the forward enumeration takes the same.

Check: the two declarations compile, and =p= points at an object of
the type the list gives.

** TODO Pointers to two integer types of one representation convert with no diagnostic

~long *p; long long *q = p;~ compiles, and so does an assignment of
an ~enum a *~ to an ~enum b *~ where both have one integer type:
=long= and =long long= are one type in the tree, and an enumerated
type is its integer type (=shape.md= chapter 3). 6.5.17.2p1 asks
compatible pointee types, and =clang= rejects both programs.

Fix: none taken; either the documents say these compile, with the
implementation's choices, or an integer type carries its spelling
for the compatibility check alone.

Check: both programs fail, or the documents say they compile.

** TODO =offsetof= draws "undeclared"

~offsetof(struct s, b)~ fails with "'__builtin_offsetof'
undeclared": the header defines the macro by the compiler's builtin,
which rcc does not know by name. The program is valid, and the
message calls it wrong.

Fix: until structs are in, =__builtin_offsetof= is refused as a
not-yet, by its name, with the builtins of =stdatomic.h=; with them,
it is the member's offset, an integer constant expression.

Check: a row of =tests/error= pinned "not yet", then a static
assertion on an offset that compiles.

** TODO A static assertion in a struct's member list is not checked

~struct s { int a; _Static_assert(0, "m"); };~ compiles: the parser
skips a member list whole, the assertion with it (6.7.3.2 lists a
static assertion among a struct's member declarations).

Fix: the slice that reads member lists checks it.

Check: the declaration fails with "static assertion failed: m".

** TODO A cast to a struct says not yet

~(struct S) x~ fails with "not yet: struct". The cast is not waiting
for a slice: C takes only =void= or a scalar type in a cast, so "cast
to a type that is not a scalar", which the tree has, is the
diagnosis. A cast to a floating type is valid C, and "not yet" is
right for it; a cast to an enum compiles.

Fix: =cast()= refuses a struct or a union as not a scalar, before it
asks whether the type is one rcc does not use yet.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with ~(struct S) x~, pinned "cast
to a type that is not a scalar".

** TODO A ~?:~ with one void arm is refused where =clang= takes it

~c ? f() : g()~ with ~f~ returning =void= and ~g~ an =int= fails
with "void and non-void operands in conditional expression".
6.5.16p3 allows no such pair, but =gcc= and =clang= take it as GNU C,
the result =void=, with a warning only under =-pedantic=; a header
written for them may have it.

Fix: decide: the error stays, as the constraint has it, or the pair
is taken as the two compilers take it, the other arm for its effect.

Check: the pin of =conditional-void-arm= agrees with the decision.

** TODO The operand of =!= is not promoted

=!E= is defined as =(0==E)= (6.5.4.3p5), and the equality operator
promotes a =char= operand to =int=. The parser builds =!c= on the
unpromoted =char=, and c1 tests it at the =char='s width, =cmpb $0,
-1(%rbp)= and =tst w0, #0xff=. The result is the same, 0 or 1, since
an extension changes no zero, but the tree does not say what the
standard says.

With the promotion cast inserted by the parser and nothing else
changed, c1 extends the value before it tests it: =movsbl -1(%rbp),
%eax; cmpl $0, %eax=, and =uxtb w0, w0; cmp w0, #0= after a call. Over
=tests= 285 functions gain an instruction on x86-64 and 15 on
aarch64, and none loses one.

Fix: none taken; decide. Either the parser promotes and both
generators test a widened narrow value against zero at its narrow
width, which then also holds for a ~(int) c == 0~ the source
writes; or =!= stays on the unpromoted operand, as a condition is
tested, and =shape.md= says so.

Check: under the first, no function longer over =tests=; under the
second, the sentence in =shape.md= chapter 5.

** TODO The comment on a local's declaration says 2 GiB

The comment on =init_declarator()= says "A local of 2 GiB or more is
refused here". The test under it is against the target's frame limit,
which is 16 bytes under 2 GiB on x86-64 and 16 bytes under 16 MiB on
aarch64: =char a[20000000];= as a local compiles with =-target
x86-64= and fails with =-target aarch64=, "variable 'a' is too large
for a local", status 1.

Fix: the comment says a local over the target's largest frame is
refused, and names no number.

Check: the comment read against =src/target.hh=.

** TODO =convert_to()= is not the one place a conversion enters the tree

The comment on =convert_to()= in =src/parse.cc= calls it "The one
place a conversion enters the tree". =decayed()= makes a cast node of
its own, for an array's decay, which =src/ast.hh= calls "a cast too".
The cast the source writes, made in =cast()=, the
comment does allow for.

Fix: the comment says every conversion but an array's decay.

Check: =grep -n 'cast_t {' src/parse.cc= prints the sites the
comments name.

** TODO An error at the end of the file names a line the file does not have

A function body the file ends inside is reported at the end-of-file
token: =tests/error/unclosed-function-body.c= has four lines, and
the error is at 5:1. =clang= reports the same program at the end of
the last token, 4:14. A reader, or an editor following the position,
is sent past the text.

Fix: the end-of-file token takes the position just after the last
token; or an error at end of file is reported at the token before
it.

Check: the pins of =unclosed-function-body= and =unclosed-block=
name the last line of their programs.

** TODO A void initializer is reported at the ===

~int x = g();~ with =g= returning =void= fails with "void expression
used as a value" at the ===, not at the call: =init_declarator()=
hands the === token to =assignment_conversion()=, which is the right
place for a conversion's error and the wrong one for an operand that
has no value.

Fix: the check for a value reports at the initializer's first token.

Check: the pin of =tests/error/void-value= names the column of =g=.

** TODO Sixteen lines of the documents run past the margin

The documents are wrapped at about 70 columns. Sixteen lines of
prose are not, a sentence added to a paragraph and not wrapped with
it: six in =docs/shape.md=, three each in =aarch64.md= and =cost.md=,
two in =design-calls.md=, one each in =backend.md= and =findings.md=.
The rows of a table are one line each by their form and do not
count.

Fix: each paragraph wrapped again, no word changed.

Check: no line of =docs/*.md= outside a table or a listing is longer
than 78 columns.

* Lexer

** TODO A binary or a bit-precise constant is an invalid integer constant

=0b101= and =1wb= are C23's (6.4.4.2). The parser has a "not yet:
binary constant" and a "not yet: bit-precise integer constant" for
them, and an error for a suffix it does not know, but none of the
three is reached: the lexer's =number()= stops at the =b= and at the
=wb= and refuses each first, "invalid integer constant '0b101'". A
valid constant is thus called invalid, and the parser's three
branches are dead.

Fix: the lexer takes the binary prefix with its digits and the =wb=
suffix as part of the number, so the parser's not-yets say what is
missing; or the lexer says "not yet" itself and the parser's
branches go.

Check: two programs of =tests/error=, pinned "not yet: binary
constant" and "not yet: bit-precise integer constant".

** TODO A digit separator is a missing quote

~1'000~ is C23's (6.4.4.2). The parser's "not yet: digit separator"
is never reached: rcc runs =clang -E= with no =-std=, clang's default
dialect has no separator, so clang warns of an open character
constant, and the lexer, which ends the number at the quote, reports
"missing terminating ' character".

Fix: decide the dialect rcc preprocesses in. With =-std=c23= passed
to =clang -E= and the quote taken by =number()=, the not-yet is
reached; without, the parser's branch goes.

Check: a program of =tests/error= with ~1'000~ and its pin.

